{{afc comment|Wikidata entry [[d:Q2174956]] Wikidata name: non-working day [[User:Immanuelle|Immanuelle]] ([[User talk:Immanuelle|talk]]) 05:23, 12 June 2025 (UTC)}} {{afc comment|Wikidata entry (none) Wikidata name: (none) [[User:Immanuelle|Immanuelle]] ([[User talk:Immanuelle|talk]]) 08:35, 11 June 2025 (UTC)}} {{Multiple issues |ref = April 2019 |original research = April 2019 }}
'''Holiday''' (休日, ''Kyūjitsu'') refers to a "day off" <ref name="daijisen_jitu">[[:en:Digital Daijisen|Digital Daijisen]] ([[Shogakukan]]), entry for "Holiday"</ref> when work, classes, or other regular duties are suspended <ref name="daijisen_jitu" /><ref name="kojien6_jitu">[[:en:Kojien|Kojien]], Sixth Edition ([[:en:Iwanami Shoten|Iwanami Shoten]]), entry for "Holiday"</ref>. According to the [[:en:Kojien|Kojien]] dictionary, holidays specifically include [[:en:Sunday|Sunday]]s and [[:en:National Holidays|National Holidays]] (≒ legally designated public holidays in various countries).
The term "'''Vacation''' (休暇, ''Kyūka'')" refers to days off granted by schools, companies, or government offices, particularly those that are not Sundays or public holidays <ref name="daijisen_ka">Digital Daijisen, entry for "Vacation"</ref><ref name="kojien6_ka">Kojien, Sixth Edition, entry for "Vacation"</ref>.
In [[:en:Japanese language|Japanese]], days when work or classes are suspended are referred to as "Kyūjitsu" (holidays), while days when work or classes take place are called "Heijitsu" (平日, weekdays). In [[:en:English language|English]], the term [[:en:Weekend|weekend]] is used for holidays occurring at the end of the week, while [[:en:Weekday|weekday]] refers to Monday through Friday.
== Overview == Holidays are determined by governments or municipalities, including weekly "rest days" such as [[:en:Sunday|Sunday]]s. Many countries also designate specific holidays such as [[:en:Independence Day|Independence Day]]. In the private sector, holidays like "founding anniversaries" are commonly observed.
Private companies may designate holidays on weekdays if their business thrives on weekends, often referred to as "weekday holidays." In some cases, "special days" (特日, ''tokujitsu'') are used as substitutes for "holidays" (citation needed). For example, [[:en:libraries|libraries]] in [[:en:Japan|Japan]] often remain open on weekends while closing on weekdays.
== Global Holidays and Vacations == === Annual Leave by Country === {| class="wikitable" style="font-size:90%;margin:1em" ! Country ! Statutory Leave Days |- |{{FRA}} |7 weeks (5 weeks plus 2 weeks in a variable holiday system) |- |{{SWE}} |5 weeks |- |{{AUT}} |5 weeks; older workers, 6 weeks |- |{{FIN}} |5 weeks |- |{{IRL}} |4 weeks plus 9 public holidays |- |{{GER}} |24 days excluding Sundays, plus 9-13 public holidays |- |{{ESP}} |Calendar-based 30 days |- |{{PER}} |Calendar-based 30 days |- |{{TUN}} |30 working days |- |{{EUR}} |4 weeks, with some countries granting more |- |{{NED}} |4 weeks |- |{{SUI}} |4 weeks |- |{{CZE}} |4 weeks |- |{{NZL}} |4 weeks (effective April 1, 2007) |- |{{AUS}} |Typically 4 weeks, with long-term paid leave standard |- |{{GBR}} |20 days plus 8 public holidays |- |{{NOR}} |25 working days |- |{{UKR}} |Calendar-based 24 days |- |{{RSA}} |21 consecutive days |- |{{BEL}} |20 paid days |- |{{BRA}} |Calendar-based 30 days |- |{{POL}} |20 weekdays; 26 days for 10 years of service |- |{{BUR}} |20 weekdays |- |{{HUN}} |20 working days |- |{{ROU}} |Minimum of 20 working days |- |{{JPN}} |10 days for workers employed for 6 months; additional days are accrued annually |- |{{BHS}} |2 weeks after 1 year of service; 3 weeks after 5 years |- |{{CHI}} |15 working days |- |{{PRI}} |15 days |- |{{KSA}} |15 days |- |{{VEN}} |15 paid days |- |{{PAR}} |2 weeks |- |{{COL}} |2 weeks |- |{{ECU}} |2 weeks |- |{{URU}} |2 weeks |- |{{ISR}} |14 days |- |{{ARG}} |12 calendar days |- |{{TUR}} |12 working days |- |{{VEN}} |10 working days |- |{{CAN}} |10 working days; determined by regional governments |- |{{USA}} |No statutory requirement; typically 7–21 days |- |{{KOR}} |10 working days |- |{{MEX}} |1 week |- |{{HKG}} |7 days |- |{{SIN}} |7 days |- |{{TWN}} |7 days |- |{{CHN}} |5 days for 1 year of service; 10 days for 10 years; 15 days for 20+ years |}
=== Revolutionary Calendar Holidays === Regions that implemented [[Revolutionary calendars]] experienced significant shifts in the definition of holidays due to the abolition of seven-day [[:en:weeks|weeks]]: * In the [[:en:French Revolutionary Calendar|French Revolutionary Calendar]], months consisted of 30 days, with the 10th, 20th, 30th day, and 5–6 additional days at the year's end designated as holidays. * The [[Soviet Union Calendar]] initially featured a five-day week (Yellow, Pink, Red, Purple, Green days) with staggered personal holidays. Later, holidays were fixed to the 6th, 12th, 18th, 24th, and 30th days of each month.
== Public Holidays in Japan == The current Japanese legal framework for "holidays" is governed by several laws, including: - The Act on National Holidays, enacted in [[:en:1948|1948]]. - The Act on Holidays of Agencies of the Diet. - The Act on Holidays of Courts (see: [[s:裁判所の休日に関する法律]]). - The Act on Holidays of Administrative Agencies.{{Refnest|[https://laws.e-gov.go.jp/law/363AC0000000091 Act on Holidays of Administrative Agencies] - [[e-gov.go.jp]]}}
A separate section covers Japanese local and national holidays in detail.
